# Illustration accessibility

Make sure everyone gets the same meaning, whether or not they can see the illustration. This applies on guidance pages, dashboards and social posts.

<h2 id="bg-ill-06">Alternative text</h2>

Decide whether the illustration adds information or repeats the text beside it. [Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) owns the wording of alternative text.

### When it adds information

Describe what the text does not already say, such as “Floor plan: the temperature sensor is on the hall wall, away from the radiator.” A complex diagram also needs a nearby explanation or a table.

Give an informative inline SVG a `title`, a `desc` and `role="img"`. This example does that. Its caption says that a real sensor needs the manufacturer’s placement instructions.

<%= example('bg-illustration-sensor-placement') %>

### When it repeats the text

Give it an empty alternative (`alt=""`). Never leave out the `alt` attribute. Hide a decorative inline SVG with `aria-hidden="true"`.

This example shows a phone, Home Assistant, a hub and a sensor. The drawing is hidden from screen readers because the sentence underneath says the same thing.

<%= example('bg-illustration-network') %>

### In Home Assistant

Give a picture card its alternative text with the `alt_text` option.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/illustrations/hall-sensor.svg
alt_text: "Floor plan: the temperature sensor is on the hall wall, away from the radiator."
```

If the card next to it already says the same thing, use `alt_text: ""`.

## Text and colour

Keep labels, steps and values as HTML text, not inside the image, so they can be read aloud, translated and resized.

Do not rely on colour alone to show which object matters. Use shape, size or position as well. [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html#using-contrast) explains how to check contrast and colour blindness.

## Layout and motion

- Keep the illustration and its text together on narrow screens.
- Give a row of panels a clear reading order and matching text.
- Do not animate a scene to attract attention.
- If a process needs animation, provide a still version and a way to pause it, and respect the reduced motion setting.
